text:
anika: Heads-up — running into intermittent parse failures in the browser SSE/fetch client. Error shows up as 'Unexpected end of JSON input' when the server stream contains emojis/non-ascii. Only happens when the client parses chunks immediately. Any quick tips?

matt: Are you using window.EventSource or fetch() + ReadableStream?

anika: fetch + stream reader + TextDecoder. I was calling JSON.parse on each chunk as it arrived (naive). The failures line up with chunk boundaries.

sasha: That is very likely multi-byte UTF-8 sequences getting split across TCP frames. TextDecoder can handle that if you use it properly with { stream: true }. If you decode raw Uint8Array chunks independently you can lose bytes.

kai: Also, the framing strategy matters — is the server sending newline-delimited JSON, SSE "data: ...\n\n", or arbitrary bytes? If there is no explicit frame delimiter you'll have to reassemble.

anika: Server is currently flushing JSON objects separated by a single blank line (basically NDJSON but with double-newline). But the client sees half an emoji in one chunk and throws on parse.

matt: Quick pattern that works in browsers: use a persistent buffer string + TextDecoder with stream:true, then try to extract complete frames using your delimiter. Pseudocode:

```js
const decoder = new TextDecoder('utf-8');
let buf = '';
const reader = res.body.getReader();
while (true) {
  const { done, value } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  buf += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
  // frames separated by "\n\n"
  let idx;
  while ((idx = buf.indexOf('\n\n')) !== -1) {
    const frame = buf.slice(0, idx).trim();
    buf = buf.slice(idx + 2);
    if (frame) {
      try {
        const obj = JSON.parse(frame);
        handle(obj);
      } catch (e) {
        // if parse fails, leave it in buf and wait for more data
        // but normally decoder + delimiter prevents mid-codepoint splits
        break;
      }
    }
  }
}
```

sasha: Exactly. Two gotchas: (1) always decode with { stream: true } so decoder buffers partial codepoints; (2) don't treat each Uint8Array as an independent string.

kai: For backpressure, don't let handle(obj) queue unbounded. Implement a bounded queue / concurrency limit. In browser you can simply stop calling reader.read() until the queue drops below a threshold (i.e., await when queue.length > MAX).

matt: In Node you can pause the stream (readable.pause()) or use an async iterator with a concurrency limiter. Also consider using a Transform stream that backpressures upstream when the consumer is slow.

anika: Example backpressure pattern I used: maintain a processing counter and if processing >= 8 then await a Promise that resolves when count drops. Simple semaphore.

kai: If you control the server, the robust fix is to add explicit frame boundaries that survive chunking: either NDJSON (one JSON per line), length-prefix (send length before payload), or use SSE 'data:' lines and ensure '\n' between events. All survive arbitrary TCP splitting because you reassemble until the delimiter.

sasha: For tests, simulate split multi-byte sequences. Make a unit test that takes a UTF-8 encoded blob and slices it in the middle of a multi-byte sequence, feed the pieces to your decoder code, and assert the decoder output equals the original string once combined.

matt: A note on retries/auth: if you get a 401 mid-stream you'll often see the connection close; ensure your client treats partial/closed streams as retriable only if ops are idempotent, and include resume tokens or an event id if you need exactly-once semantics.

anika: Great — I'll switch to decoder.decode(..., {stream:true}) + NDJSON parsing + a semaphore for concurrency. Also adding the unit that splits emoji bytes.

deploy-bot: deploy-check: sse-client-tests — started 

anika: Thanks all :thumbsup: I'll open a PR with the fix and a small test harness.

matt: If you want I can review the PR and poke at the Node client too.

anika: Please do. Shipping change in next patch release.
